  "body": "Split from #131 (Support Tormach machines). PR #162 noted it as a possible follow-up: \"G84 on non-MX machines: non-MX 770s (R8 spindle) may not rigid-tap. Telling those machines apart needs a capability flag in the machine file.\"\n\n**What happens today:** the Tormach PathPilot post writes `G84` rigid tapping for any Tormach machine file. On a machine whose spindle can't rigid-tap (an R8 non-MX 770 without the needed spindle feedback), the program would be wrong for that machine, with no warning.\n\n**Expected:**\n- A machine-file capability, for example `rigid_tapping = true|false`. Either it defaults to false and the MX files set it true, or it's explicit on every Tormach file; record the choice.\n- The post gate (or the post) **refuses a Tap op by name** on a machine without it. It never posts G84 there, and never silently converts it to a different cycle.\n- The Machines pane shows the capability.\n- Other posts and machines are unaffected, unless a default is chosen that every built-in file must state.\n\n**Sources:** Tormach's public manuals for which models and spindles support rigid tapping. Behaviour only, per the Clean-Room Protocol. The 770MX manual (UM10587) chapter 10 covers G84 on the MX.\n\nEffort: high, under the safety floor (it decides whether posted G-code is written).\n\n**Done means:**\n- The capability is in the `.mch` schema, validated and shown in the Machines pane.\n- Every Tormach machine file states it correctly, from the manuals.\n- A Tap op on a non-capable machine is refused by name, pinned by a test.\n- The 770MX G84 golden is unchanged.\n\nPart of #131.\n",
